          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7.2.3 Analysing Statistical Data Using Probability"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.299,
            0.885,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The way of collecting evidence through statistical data is used extensively in the world of business for marketing, forecasting sales, insurances and in science and social science research."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.362,
            0.886,
            0.44
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 5:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Suppose you anonymously collect information regarding the favourite fruit of 50 students in your class. Let us assume that the results are: 20 students like mango, 15 students like apples, 10 students like bananas, and 5 students like grapes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.44,
            0.886,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us play a game! Suppose we randomly pick one student from the class and try to guess their favourite fruit. What’s the probability that the student’s favourite fruit is mango? Statistical probability is based on collected data. So, a reasonable estimate that the student’s favourite fruit is mango is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.541,
            0.615,
            0.563
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Probability of mango as the favourite fruit"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.577,
            0.685,
            0.627
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_7_equation_11.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{ Number of students who like imaging }{ Total number of students } = \\frac{20}{50} = 0.4"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.643,
            0.885,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, there is a 0.4 or 40% chance that a randomly chosen student in the class likes mango."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.682,
            0.888,
            0.858
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now assume that you want to buy fruits for the entire school depending on the students’ favourite fruits. You would need to estimate the quantity of each type of fruit you need to purchase for the entire school of, say, 1500 students. It is usually impractical to collect data from all students or the entire "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "population",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The application of statistical probability in the real world is usually based on evidence collected from a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "sample",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The population, i.e., the total number of students in the school, is 1500, and we collected evidence from a sample of 50 students from one class. An estimate of the probability that mango"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
